<Legal title="Privacy" updated="21 November 2026">
	<p>
		This explains what Bagful keeps about you and who helps it do that. Bagful is a shared shopping list, so most of
		what it holds is the lists you and the people you shop with write.
	</p>

	<h2>What Bagful stores</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Your account: the sign-in details you give when you create an account, held by our sign-in provider.</li>
		<li>Your lists: list names, items, quantities, who added or ticked off what, and your purchase history.</li>
		<li>Who is on a list: members of a list can see each other's names and what each person added.</li>
		<li>Your device for notifications, if you turn them on, so a phone can be told when something changes.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Who handles it</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Sign-in is handled by Clerk.</li>
		<li>Lists and history are stored in a Supabase database.</li>
		<li>Push notifications are delivered by OneSignal.</li>
		<li>
			Usage statistics are collected with PostHog, hosted in the EU. They are used to see which parts of the app
			work and which do not, and personal details are removed before they are sent.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>What you control</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>You can leave a list, or delete it if you own it.</li>
		<li>You can turn notifications off in the app or in your phone's settings.</li>
		<li>You can ask for your account and its data to be deleted.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Selling and ads</h2>
	<p>Bagful does not sell your data and shows no ads.</p>

	<h2>Questions</h2>
	<p>
		Open an issue on <a href="https://github.com/raduandreigorcea/Bagful/issues" rel="noopener">GitHub</a> and we will
		answer there.
	</p>
</Legal>
